## an7.23
an7.23:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
an7.23:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha, on the Vulture’s Peak Mountain. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha on Vulture Peak Mountain.
an7.23:1.3 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: There he addressed the monks:
an7.23:1.4 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you these seven principles that prevent decline. | T: “Monks, I will teach you the seven conditions that lead to no decline.
an7.23:1.5 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: Listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
an7.23:1.6 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” the monks responded to the Blessed One.
an7.23:1.7 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said:
an7.23:2.1 | S: “What are the seven principles that prevent decline? | T: “And which seven are the conditions that lead to no decline?
an7.23:2.2 | S: As long as the mendicants meet frequently and have many meetings, they can expect growth, not decline. | T: [1] “As long as the monks meet often, meet a great deal, their growth can be expected, not their decline.
an7.23:3.1 | S: As long as the mendicants meet in harmony, leave in harmony, and carry on their business in harmony, they can expect growth, not decline. | T: [2] “As long as the monks meet in harmony, adjourn from their meetings in harmony, and conduct Saṅgha business in harmony, their growth can be expected, not their decline.
an7.23:4.1 | S: As long as the mendicants don’t make new decrees or abolish existing decrees, but proceed having undertaken the training rules as they have been decreed, they can expect growth, not decline. | T: [3] “As long as the monks neither decree what has been undecreed nor repeal what has been decreed, but practice undertaking the training rules as they have been decreed, their growth can be expected, not their decline.
an7.23:5.1 | S: As long as the mendicants honor, respect, esteem, and venerate the senior mendicants—of long standing, long gone forth, fathers and leaders of the Saṅgha—and think them worth listening to, they can expect growth, not decline. | T: [4] “As long as the monks honor, respect, venerate, and do homage to the elder monks—those with seniority who have long been ordained, the fathers of the Saṅgha, leaders of the Saṅgha—regarding them as worth listening to, their growth can be expected, not their decline.
an7.23:6.1 | S: As long as the mendicants don’t fall under the sway of arisen craving for future lives, they can expect growth, not decline. | T: [5] “As long as the monks do not submit to the power of any arisen craving that leads to further becoming, their growth can be expected, not their decline.
an7.23:7.1 | S: As long as the mendicants take care to live in wilderness lodgings, they can expect growth, not decline. | T: [6] “As long as the monks see their own benefit in wilderness dwellings, their growth can be expected, not their decline.
an7.23:8.1 | S: As long as the mendicants individually establish mindfulness, so that more good-hearted spiritual companions might come, and those that have already come may live comfortably, they can expect growth, not decline. | T: [7] “As long as the monks each keep firmly in mind: ‘If there are any well-behaved companions in the holy life who have yet to come, may they come; and may the well-behaved companions in the holy life who have come live in comfort,’ their growth can be expected, not their decline.
an7.23:9.1 | S: As long as these seven principles that prevent decline last among the mendicants, and as long as the mendicants are seen following them, they can expect growth, not decline.” | T: “As long as the monks remain steadfast in these seven conditions, and as long as these seven conditions endure among the monks, the monks’ growth can be expected, not their decline.”
an7.23:9.2 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an7.32
an7.32:1.1 | S: Then, late at night, a glorious deity, lighting up the entire Jeta’s Grove, went up to the Buddha, bowed, stood to one side, and said to him: | T: Then a certain devatā, in the far extreme of the night, her extreme radiance lighting up the entirety of Jeta’s Grove, went to the Blessed One. On arrival, having bowed down to him, she stood to one side. As she was standing there, she said to the Blessed One,
an7.32:2.1 | S: “Sir, these seven things don’t lead to the decline of a mendicant. | T: “These seven qualities, lord, lead to a monk’s non-decline.
an7.32:2.2 | S: What seven? | T: Which seven?
an7.32:2.3 | S: Respect for the Teacher, for the teaching, for the Saṅgha, for the training, for immersion, for diligence, and for hospitality. | T: Respect for the teacher, respect for the Dhamma, respect for the Saṅgha, respect for training, respect for concentration, respect for heedfulness, respect for hospitality.
an7.32:2.4 | S: These seven things don’t lead to the decline of a mendicant.” | T: These seven qualities, lord, lead to the non-decline of a monk.”
an7.32:2.5 | S: That is what that deity said, | T: That is what the devatā said.
an7.32:2.6 | S: and the Teacher concurred. | T: The Teacher approved.
an7.32:2.7 | S: Then that deity, knowing that the Teacher concurred, bowed and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on his right, before vanishing right there. | T: Sensing, “The Teacher approves of me,” the devatā bowed down to the Blessed One and, circled him three times, keeping him to her right, and then disappeared right there.
an7.32:3.1 | S: Then, when the night had passed, the Buddha told the mendicants all that had happened, adding: | T: Then when the night had past, The Blessed One addressed the monks:
an7.32:3.2 | P: “imaṁ, bhikkhave, rattiṁ aññatarā devatā abhikkantāya rattiyā abhikkantavaṇṇā kevalakappaṁ jetavanaṁ obhāsetvā yenāhaṁ tenupasaṅkami; upasaṅkamitvā maṁ abhivādetvā ekamantaṁ aṭṭhāsi. Ekamantaṁ ṭhitā kho, bhikkhave, sā devatā maṁ etadavoca: | T: “Last night, monks, a certain devatā in the far extreme of the night, her extreme radiance lighting up the entirety of Jeta’s Grove, came to me and, on arrival, bowed down to me and stood to one side. As she was standing there, she said to me,
an7.32:3.3 | P: ‘sattime, bhante, dhammā bhikkhuno aparihānāya saṁvattanti. | T: ‘These seven qualities, lord, lead to a monk’s non-decline.
an7.32:3.4 | P: Katame satta? | T: Which seven?
an7.32:3.5 | P: Satthugāravatā, dhammagāravatā, saṅghagāravatā, sikkhāgāravatā, samādhigāravatā, appamādagāravatā, paṭisanthāragāravatā— | T: Respect for the teacher, respect for the Dhamma, respect for the Saṅgha, respect for training, respect for concentration, respect for heedfulness, respect for hospitality.
an7.32:3.6 | P: ime kho, bhante, satta dhammā bhikkhuno aparihānāya saṁvattantī’ti. | T: These seven qualities, lord, lead to the non-decline of a monk.’
an7.32:3.7 | P: Idamavoca, bhikkhave, sā devatā. | T: “That is what that devatā said.
an7.32:3.8 | P: Idaṁ vatvā maṁ abhivādetvā padakkhiṇaṁ katvā tatthevantaradhāyīti. | T: Having said it, she bowed down to me, circled me three times, and then disappeared right there.”
an7.32:4.1 | S: “Respect for the Teacher and the teaching, | T: Respecting the Teacher respecting the Dhamma,
an7.32:4.2 | S: and keen respect for the Saṅgha; | T: and with fierce respect for the Saṅgha,
an7.32:4.3 | S: respect for immersion, being energetic, | T: respecting concentration, ardent,
an7.32:4.4 | S: and keen respect for the training. | T: and with fierce respect for training,
an7.32:5.1 | S: A mendicant who respects diligence | T: a monk respecting heedfulness,
an7.32:5.2 | S: and hospitality | T: and with respect for hospitality
an7.32:5.3 | S: can’t decline, | T: —incapable of decline—
an7.32:5.4 | S: and has drawn near to extinguishment.” | T: is right in the presence of unbinding.
an7.32:5.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an7.33
an7.33:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, tonight, a glorious deity, lighting up the entire Jeta’s Grove, came to me, bowed, stood to one side, and said to me: | T: “Last night, monks, a certain devatā in the far extreme of the night, her extreme radiance lighting up the entirety of Jeta’s Grove, came to me and, on arrival, bowed down to me and stood to one side. As she was standing there, she said to me,
an7.33:1.2 | S: ‘Sir, these seven things don’t lead to the decline of a mendicant. | T: ‘These seven qualities, lord, lead to a monk’s non-decline.
an7.33:1.3 | S: What seven? | T: Which seven?
an7.33:1.4 | S: Respect for the Teacher, for the teaching, for the Saṅgha, for the training, for immersion, for conscience, and for prudence. | T: Respect for the teacher, respect for the Dhamma, respect for the Saṅgha, respect for training, respect for concentration, respect for shame, respect for compunction.
an7.33:1.5 | S: These seven things don’t lead to the decline of a mendicant.’ | T: These seven qualities, lord, lead to a monk’s non-decline.’
an7.33:1.6 | S: That is what that deity said. | T: “That is what that devatā said.
an7.33:1.7 | S: Then he bowed and respectfully circled me, keeping me on his right side, before vanishing right there. | T: Having said it, she bowed down to me, circled me three times, and then disappeared right there.”
an7.33:2.1 | S: One respectful of the Teacher and the teaching, | T: Respecting the Teacher respecting the Dhamma,
an7.33:2.2 | S: keenly respecting the Saṅgha; | T: and with fierce respect for the Saṅgha,
an7.33:2.3 | S: respecting immersion, being energetic, | T: respecting concentration, ardent,
an7.33:2.4 | S: and keenly respecting the training; | T: and with fierce respect for training,
an7.33:3.1 | S: endowed with conscience and prudence, | T: consummate in shame & compunction,
an7.33:3.2 | S: reverential and respectful— | T: deferential, respectful
an7.33:3.3 | S: it is impossible for them to decline; | T: —incapable of decline—
an7.33:3.4 | S: they have drawn near to extinguishment.” | T: one is right in the presence of unbinding.
an7.33:3.5 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

