an7.67:13.4 | S: they are then called a noble disciple who cannot be overrun by Māra, who cannot be overrun by the Wicked One. | T: he is said to be a disciple of the noble ones who can’t be undone by Māra, can’t be undone by the Evil One.
an7.67:13.5 | S: What are the seven good qualities that they possess? | T: “Now, with which seven true qualities is he endowed?
an7.67:14.1 | S: Just as a king’s frontier citadel has a pillar with deep foundations, firmly embedded, imperturbable and unshakable, to defend those within and repel those outside, | T: “Just as the royal frontier fortress has a foundation post—deeply rooted, well embedded, immovable, & unshakable—for the protection of those within and to ward off those without;
an7.67:14.2 | S: in the same way a noble disciple has faith in the Realized One’s awakening: | T: in the same way a disciple of the noble ones has conviction, is convinced of the Tathāgata’s awakening:
an7.67:14.3 | S: ‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ | T: ‘Indeed, the Blessed One is worthy & rightly self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, well-gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed.’
an7.67:14.4 | S: A noble disciple with faith as their pillar gives up the unskillful and develops the skillful, | T: With conviction as his foundation post, the disciple of the noble ones abandons what is unskillful, develops what is skillful,
an7.67:14.5 | S: they give up the blameworthy and develop the blameless, | T: abandons what is blameworthy, develops what is blameless,
an7.67:14.6 | S: and they keep themselves pure. | T: and looks after himself with purity.
an7.67:14.7 | S: This is the first good quality they have. | T: With this first true quality is he endowed.
an7.67:15.1 | S: Just as a citadel has a moat that is deep and wide, | T: “Just as the royal frontier fortress has a moat, both deep & wide, for the protection of those within and to ward off those without;
an7.67:15.2 | S: in the same way a noble disciple has a conscience. They’re conscientious about bad conduct by way of body, speech, and mind, and conscientious about having any bad, unskillful qualities. | T: in the same way, the disciple of the noble ones has a sense of shame. He feels shame at (the thought of engaging in) bodily misconduct, verbal misconduct, mental misconduct. He feels shame at falling into evil, unskillful actions.
an7.67:15.3 | S: A noble disciple with a conscience as their moat gives up the unskillful and develops the skillful, | T: With a sense of shame as his moat, the disciple of the noble ones abandons what is unskillful, develops what is skillful,
an7.67:15.4 | S: they give up the blameworthy and develop the blameless, | T: abandons what is blameworthy, develops what is blameless,
an7.67:15.5 | S: and they keep themselves pure. | T: and looks after himself with purity.
an7.67:15.6 | S: This is the second good quality they have. | T: With this second true quality is he endowed.
an7.67:16.1 | S: Just as a citadel has a patrol path that is high and wide, | T: “Just as the royal frontier fortress has an encircling road, both high & wide, for the protection of those within and to ward off those without;
an7.67:16.2 | S: in the same way a noble disciple is prudent. They’re prudent when it comes to bad conduct by way of body, speech, and mind, and prudent when it comes to acquiring any bad, unskillful qualities. | T: in the same way, the disciple of the noble ones has a sense of compunction. He feels compunction at (the suffering that would result from) bodily misconduct, verbal misconduct, mental misconduct. He feels compunction at falling into evil, unskillful actions.
an7.67:16.3 | S: A noble disciple with prudence as their patrol path gives up the unskillful and develops the skillful, | T: With a sense of compunction as his encircling road, the disciple of the noble ones abandons what is unskillful, develops what is skillful,
an7.67:16.4 | S: they give up the blameworthy and develop the blameless, | T: abandons what is blameworthy, develops what is blameless,
an7.67:16.5 | S: and they keep themselves pure. | T: and looks after himself with purity.
an7.67:16.6 | S: This is the third good quality they have. | T: With this third true quality is he endowed.
an7.67:17.1 | S: Just as a citadel has stores of many weapons, both projectile and hand-held, | T: “Just as the royal frontier fortress has many weapons stored, both arrows & things to be hurled, for the protection of those within and to ward off those without;
an7.67:17.2 | S: in the same way a noble disciple is very learned. They remember and keep what they’ve learned. These teachings are good in the beginning, good in the middle, and good in the end, meaningful and well-phrased, describing a spiritual practice that’s entirely full and pure. They are very learned in such teachings, remembering them, reciting them, mentally scrutinizing them, and penetrating them theoretically. | T: in the same way, the disciple of the noble ones has heard much, has retained what he has heard, has stored what he has heard. Whatever teachings are admirable in the beginning, admirable in the middle, admirable in the end, that—in their meaning & expression—proclaim the holy life that is entirely perfect, surpassingly pure: Those he has listened to often, retained, discussed, accumulated, examined with his mind, and well-penetrated in terms of his views.
an7.67:17.3 | S: A noble disciple with learning as their weapon gives up the unskillful and develops the skillful, | T: With learning as his weapons, the disciple of the noble ones abandons what is unskillful, develops what is skillful,
an7.67:17.4 | S: they give up the blameworthy and develop the blameless, | T: abandons what is blameworthy, develops what is blameless,
an7.67:17.5 | S: and they keep themselves pure. | T: and looks after himself with purity.
an7.67:17.6 | S: This is the fourth good quality they have. | T: With this fourth true quality is he endowed.
an7.67:18.1 | S: Just as many kinds of armed forces reside in a citadel … | T: “Just as the royal frontier fortress has a large army stationed within—
an7.67:18.2 | P: hatthārohā assārohā rathikā dhanuggahā celakā calakā piṇḍadāyakā uggā rājaputtā pakkhandino mahānāgā sūrā cammayodhino dāsakaputtā abbhantarānaṁ guttiyā bāhirānaṁ paṭighātāya. | T: elephant soldiers, cavalry, charioteers, bowmen, standard-bearers, billeting officers, soldiers of the supply corps, noted princes, commando heroes, infantry, & slaves—for the protection of those within and to ward off those without;
an7.67:18.3 | S: in the same way a noble disciple is energetic. They live with energy roused up for giving up unskillful qualities and embracing skillful qualities. They are strong, staunchly vigorous, not slacking off when it comes to developing skillful qualities. | T: in the same way a disciple of the noble ones keeps his persistence aroused for abandoning unskillful mental qualities and taking on skillful mental qualities, is steadfast, solid in his effort, not shirking his duties with regard to skillful mental qualities.
an7.67:18.4 | S: A noble disciple with energy as their armed forces gives up the unskillful and develops the skillful, | T: With persistence as his army, the disciple of the noble ones abandons what is unskillful, develops what is skillful,
an7.67:18.5 | S: they give up the blameworthy and develop the blameless, | T: abandons what is blameworthy, develops what is blameless,
an7.67:18.6 | S: and they keep themselves pure. | T: and looks after himself with purity.
an7.67:18.7 | S: This is the fifth good quality they have. | T: With this fifth true quality is he endowed.
an7.67:19.1 | S: Just as a citadel has a gatekeeper who is astute, competent, and intelligent, who keeps strangers out and lets known people in, | T: “Just as the royal frontier fortress has a gatekeeper—wise, competent, intelligent—to keep out those he doesn’t know and to let in those he does, for the protection of those within and to ward off those without;
an7.67:19.2 | S: in the same way a noble disciple is mindful. They have utmost mindfulness and alertness, and can remember and recall what was said and done long ago. | T: in the same way a disciple of the noble ones is mindful, endowed with excellent proficiency in mindfulness, remembering & able to call to mind even things that were done & said long ago.
an7.67:19.3 | S: A noble disciple with mindfulness as their gatekeeper gives up the unskillful and develops the skillful, | T: With mindfulness as his gatekeeper, the disciple of the noble ones abandons what is unskillful, develops what is skillful,
an7.67:19.4 | S: they give up the blameworthy and develop the blameless, | T: abandons what is blameworthy, develops what is blameless,
an7.67:19.5 | S: and they keep themselves pure. | T: and looks after himself with purity.
an7.67:19.6 | S: This is the sixth good quality they have. | T: With this sixth true quality is he endowed.
an7.67:20.1 | S: Just as a citadel has a wall that’s high and wide, covered with plaster, to defend those within and repel those outside, | T: “Just as the royal frontier fortress has ramparts—high & thick & completely covered with plaster—for the protection of those within and to ward off those without;
an7.67:20.2 | S: in the same way a noble disciple is wise. They have the wisdom of arising and passing away which is noble, penetrative, and leads to the complete ending of suffering. | T: in the same way a disciple of the noble ones is discerning, endowed with discernment leading to the arising of the goal—noble, penetrating, leading to the right ending of stress.
an7.67:20.3 | S: A noble disciple with wisdom as their wall gives up the unskillful and develops the skillful, | T: With discernment as his covering of plaster, the disciple of the noble ones abandons what is unskillful, develops what is skillful,
an7.67:20.4 | S: they give up the blameworthy and develop the blameless, | T: abandons what is blameworthy, develops what is blameless,
an7.67:20.5 | S: and they keep themselves pure. | T: and looks after himself with purity.
an7.67:20.6 | S: This is the seventh good quality they have. | T: With this seventh true quality is he endowed.
an7.67:20.7 | S: These are the seven good qualities that they possess. | T: “These are the seven true qualities with which he is endowed.
an7.67:21.1 | S: And what are the four absorptions—blissful meditations in this life that belong to the higher mind—that they get when they want, without trouble or difficulty? | T: “And which are the four jhānas—heightened mental states that provide a pleasant abiding in the here & now—that he can obtain at will, without difficulty, without trouble?
an7.67:21.2 | S: Just as a king’s frontier citadel has much hay, wood, and water stored up for the enjoyment, relief, and comfort of those within and to repel those outside, | T: “Just as a royal frontier fortress has large stores of grass, timber & water for the delight, convenience, & comfort of those within, and to ward off those without;
an7.67:21.3 | S: in the same way a noble disciple, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters and remains in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: in the same way the disciple of the noble ones, quite secluded from sensuality, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters & remains in the first jhāna—rapture & pleasure born of seclusion, accompanied by directed thought & evaluation—
an7.67:21.4 | S: This is for their own enjoyment, relief, and comfort, and for alighting upon extinguishment. | T: for his own delight, convenience, & comfort, and to alight on unbinding.
an7.67:22.1 | S: Just as a king’s frontier citadel has much rice and barley stored up, | T: “Just as a royal frontier fortress has large stores of rice & barley for the delight, convenience, & comfort of those within, and to ward off those without;
an7.67:22.2 | S: in the same way, as the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, a noble disciple enters and remains in the second absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of immersion, with internal clarity and mind at one, without placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: in the same way the disciple of the noble ones, with the stilling of directed thoughts & evaluations, enters & remains in the second jhāna—rapture & pleasure born of concentration, unification of awareness free from directed thought & evaluation—internal assurance—
an7.67:22.3 | S: This is for their own enjoyment, relief, and comfort, and for alighting upon extinguishment. | T: for his own delight, convenience, & comfort, and to alight on unbinding.
an7.67:23.1 | S: Just as a king’s frontier citadel has much food such as sesame, green gram, and black gram stored up, | T: “Just as a royal frontier fortress has large stores of sesame, green gram, & other beans for the delight, convenience, & comfort of those within, and to ward off those without;
an7.67:23.2 | S: in the same way with the fading away of rapture, a noble disciple enters and remains in the third absorption, where they meditate with equanimity, mindful and aware, experiencing for themselves the bliss of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous and mindful, one meditates in bliss.’ | T: in the same way the disciple of the noble ones, with the fading of rapture, remains equanimous, mindful, & alert, and senses pleasure with the body. He enters & remains in the third jhāna—of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous & mindful, he has a pleasant abiding’—

