an7.67:23.3 | S: This is for their own enjoyment, relief, and comfort, and for alighting upon extinguishment. | T: for his own delight, convenience, & comfort, and to alight on unbinding.
an7.67:24.1 | S: Just as a king’s frontier citadel has much medicine— | T: “Just as a royal frontier fortress has large stores of tonics—
an7.67:24.2 | S: ghee, butter, oil, honey, molasses, and salt—stored up for the enjoyment, relief, and comfort of those within and to repel those outside, | T: ghee, fresh butter, oil, honey, molasses, & salt—for the delight, convenience, & comfort of those within, and to ward off those without;
an7.67:24.3 | S: in the same way, with the giving up of pleasure and pain and the disappearance of former happiness and sadness, a noble disciple enters and remains in the fourth absorption, without pleasure or pain, with pure equanimity and mindfulness. | T: in the same way the disciple of the noble ones, with the abandoning of pleasure & pain, as with the earlier disappearance of elation & distress, enters & remains in the fourth jhāna—purity of equanimity & mindfulness, neither pleasure nor pain—
an7.67:24.4 | S: This is for their own enjoyment, relief, and comfort, and for alighting upon extinguishment. | T: for his own delight, convenience, & comfort, and to alight on unbinding.
an7.67:24.5 | S: These are the four absorptions—blissful meditations in this life that belong to the higher mind—which they get when they want, without trouble or difficulty. | T: “These are the four jhānas—heightened mental states that provide a pleasant abiding in the here & now—that he can obtain at will, without difficulty, without trouble.
an7.67:25.1 | S: When a noble disciple has seven good qualities, and they get the four absorptions—blissful meditations in this life that belong to the higher mind—when they want, without trouble or difficulty, | T: “When a disciple of the noble ones is endowed with these seven true qualities and can obtain at will—without difficulty, without trouble—these four jhānas, heightened mental states that provide a pleasant abiding in the here & now,
an7.67:25.2 | S: they are then called a noble disciple who cannot be overrun by Māra, who cannot be overrun by the Wicked One.” | T: he is said to be a disciple of the noble ones who can’t be undone by Māra, can’t be undone by the Evil One.”
an7.67:25.3 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an7.68
an7.68:1.1 | S: “A mendicant with seven qualities is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of veneration with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world. | T: “A monk endowed with these seven qualities is deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, an unexcelled field of merit for the world.
an7.68:1.2 | S: What seven? | T: Which seven?
an7.68:1.3 | S: It’s when a mendicant knows the teachings, knows the meaning, has self-knowledge, knows moderation, knows the right time, knows assemblies, and knows individuals high and low. | T: There is the case where a monk is one with a sense of Dhamma, a sense of meaning, a sense of himself, a sense of moderation, a sense of time, a sense of social gatherings, & a sense of distinctions among individuals.
an7.68:2.1 | S: And how is a mendicant one who knows the teachings? | T: “And how is a monk one with a sense of Dhamma?
an7.68:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant knows the teachings: | T: There is the case where a monk knows the Dhamma:
an7.68:2.3 | S: statements, mixed prose & verse, discussions, verses, inspired exclamations, legends, stories of past lives, amazing stories, and elaborations. | T: dialogues, narratives of mixed prose and verse, explanations, verses, spontaneous exclamations, quotations, birth stories, amazing events, question & answer sessions [the earliest classifications of the Buddha’s teachings].
an7.68:2.4 | S: If a mendicant did not know these teachings, | T: If he didn’t know the Dhamma—dialogues, narratives of mixed prose and verse, explanations, verses, spontaneous exclamations, quotations, birth stories, amazing events, question & answer sessions—
an7.68:2.5 | S: they would not be called ‘one who knows the teachings’. | T: he wouldn’t be said to be one with a sense of Dhamma.
an7.68:2.6 | S: But because they do know these teachings, | T: So it’s because he does know the Dhamma—dialogues… question & answer sessions—
an7.68:2.7 | S: they are called ‘one who knows the teachings’. | T: that he is said to be one with a sense of Dhamma.
an7.68:2.8 | S: Such is the one who knows the teachings. | T: This is one with a sense of Dhamma.
an7.68:3.1 | S: And how are they one who knows the meaning? | T: “And how is a monk one with a sense of meaning?
an7.68:3.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant knows the meaning of this or that statement: | T: There is the case where a monk knows the meaning of this & that statement—
an7.68:3.3 | S: ‘This is what that statement means; that is what this statement means.’ | T: ‘This is the meaning of that statement; that is the meaning of this.’
an7.68:3.4 | S: If a mendicant did not know the meaning of this or that statement, | T: If he didn’t know the meaning of this & that statement—‘This is the meaning of that statement; that is the meaning of this’—
an7.68:3.5 | S: they would not be called ‘one who knows the meaning’. | T: he wouldn’t be said to be one with a sense of meaning.
an7.68:3.6 | S: But because they do know the meaning of this or that statement, | T: So it’s because he does know the meaning of this & that statement—‘This is the meaning of that statement; that is the meaning of this’—
an7.68:3.7 | S: they are called ‘one who knows the meaning’. | T: that he is said to be one with a sense of meaning.
an7.68:3.8 | S: Such is the one who knows the teachings and the one who knows the meaning. | T: This is one with a sense of Dhamma & a sense of meaning.
an7.68:4.1 | S: And how are they one who has self-knowledge? | T: “And how is a monk one with a sense of himself?
an7.68:4.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant has self-knowledge: | T: There is the case where a monk knows himself:
an7.68:4.3 | S: ‘This is the extent of my faith, ethics, learning, generosity, wisdom, and eloquence.’ | T: ‘This is how far I have come in conviction, virtue, learning, generosity, discernment, quick-wittedness.’
an7.68:4.4 | S: If a mendicant did not have self-knowledge, | T: If he didn’t know himself—‘This is how far I have come in conviction, virtue, learning, generosity, discernment, quick-wittedness’—
an7.68:4.5 | S: they would not be called ‘one who has self-knowledge’. | T: he wouldn’t be said to be one with a sense of himself.
an7.68:4.6 | S: But because they do have self-knowledge, | T: So it’s because he does know himself—‘This is how far I have come in conviction, virtue, learning, generosity, discernment, quick-wittedness’—
an7.68:4.7 | S: they are called ‘one who has self-knowledge’. | T: that he is said to be one with a sense of himself.
an7.68:4.8 | S: Such is the one who knows the teachings, the one who knows the meaning, and the one who has self-knowledge. | T: This is one with a sense of Dhamma, a sense of meaning, & a sense of himself.
an7.68:5.1 | S: And how are they one who knows moderation? | T: “And how is a monk one with a sense of moderation?
an7.68:5.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant knows moderation when receiving robes, almsfood, lodgings, and medicines and supplies for the sick. | T: There is the case where a monk knows moderation in accepting robes, almsfood, lodgings, & medicinal requisites for curing the sick.
an7.68:5.3 | S: If a mendicant did not know moderation, they would not be called ‘one who knows moderation’. | T: If he didn’t know moderation in accepting robes, almsfood, lodgings, & medicinal requisites for curing the sick, he wouldn’t be said to be one with a sense of moderation.
an7.68:5.4 | S: But because they do know moderation, they are called ‘one who knows moderation’. | T: So it’s because he does know moderation in accepting robes, almsfood, lodgings, & medicinal requisites for curing the sick, that he is said to be one with a sense of moderation.
an7.68:5.5 | S: Such is the one who knows the teachings, the one who knows the meaning, the one who has self-knowledge, and the one who knows moderation. | T: This is one with a sense of Dhamma, a sense of meaning, a sense of himself, & a sense of moderation.
an7.68:6.1 | S: And how are they one who knows the right time? | T: “And how is a monk one with a sense of time?
an7.68:6.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant knows the right time: | T: There is the case where a monk knows the time:
an7.68:6.3 | S: ‘This is the time for recitation; this is the time for questioning; this is the time for meditation; this is the time for retreat.’ | T: ‘This is the time for recitation; this, the time for questioning; this, the time for making an effort (in meditation); this, the time for seclusion.’
an7.68:6.4 | S: If a mendicant did not know the right time, | T: If he didn’t know the time—‘This is the time for recitation; this, the time for questioning; this, the time for making an effort; this, the time for seclusion’—
an7.68:6.5 | S: they would not be called ‘one who knows the right time’. | T: he wouldn’t be said to be one with a sense of time.
an7.68:6.6 | S: But because they do know the right time, | T: So it’s because he does know the time—‘This is the time for recitation; this, the time for questioning; this, the time for making an effort; this, the time for seclusion’—
an7.68:6.7 | S: they are called ‘one who knows the right time’. | T: that he is said to be one with a sense of time.
an7.68:6.8 | S: Such is the one who knows the teachings, the one who knows the meaning, the one who has self-knowledge, the one who knows moderation, and the one who knows the right time. | T: This is one with a sense of Dhamma, a sense of meaning, a sense of himself, a sense of moderation, & a sense of time.
an7.68:7.1 | S: And how are they one who knows assemblies? | T: “And how is a monk one with a sense of social gatherings?
an7.68:7.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant knows assemblies: | T: There is the case where a monk knows his social gathering:
an7.68:7.3 | S: ‘This is an assembly of aristocrats, of brahmins, of householders, or of ascetics. | T: ‘This is a social gathering of noble warriors; this, a social gathering of brahmans; this, a social gathering of householders; this, a social gathering of contemplatives;
an7.68:7.4 | S: This one should be approached in this way. This is how to stand, to act, to sit, to speak, or to stay silent when there.’ | T: here one should approach them in this way, stand in this way, act in this way, sit in this way, speak in this way, stay silent in this way.’
an7.68:7.5 | S: If a mendicant did not know assemblies, | T: If he didn’t know his social gathering—
an7.68:7.6 | P: ‘ayaṁ khattiyaparisā …pe… | T: ‘This is a social gathering of noble warriors; this, a social gathering of brahmans; this, a social gathering of householders; this, a social gathering of contemplatives; here one should approach them in this way, stand in this way, act in this way, sit in this way, speak in this way, stay silent in this way’—
an7.68:7.7 | S: they would not be called ‘one who knows assemblies’. | T: he wouldn’t be said to be one with a sense of social gatherings.
an7.68:7.8 | S: But because they do know assemblies, | T: So it’s because he does know his social gathering—
an7.68:7.9 | P: ‘ayaṁ khattiyaparisā, ayaṁ brāhmaṇaparisā, ayaṁ gahapatiparisā, ayaṁ samaṇaparisā. | T: ‘This is a social gathering of noble warriors; this, a social gathering of brahmans; this, a social gathering of householders; this, a social gathering of contemplatives; here one should approach them in this way, stand in this way, act in this way, sit in this way, speak in this way, stay silent in this way’—
an7.68:7.10 | S: they are called ‘one who knows assemblies’. | T: that he is said to be one with a sense of social gatherings.
an7.68:7.11 | S: Such is the one who knows the teachings, the one who knows the meaning, the one who has self-knowledge, the one who knows moderation, the one who knows the right time, and the one who knows assemblies. | T: This is one with a sense of Dhamma, a sense of meaning, a sense of himself, a sense of moderation, a sense of time, & a sense of social gatherings.
an7.68:8.1 | S: And how are they one who knows individuals high and low? | T: “And how is a monk one with a sense of distinctions among individuals?
an7.68:8.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant understands individuals in terms of pairs. | T: There is the case where people are known to a monk in terms of two categories.
an7.68:8.3 | S: Two individuals: | T: “Of two people—
an7.68:8.4 | S: one likes to see the noble ones, one does not. | T: one who wants to see noble ones and one who doesn’t—
an7.68:8.5 | S: The individual who doesn’t like to see the noble ones | T: the one who doesn’t want to see noble ones
an7.68:8.6 | S: is reprehensible in that respect. | T: is to be criticized for that reason,
an7.68:8.7 | S: The individual who does like to see the noble ones | T: the one who does want to see noble ones is,
an7.68:8.8 | S: is praiseworthy in that respect. | T: for that reason, to be praised.
an7.68:9.1 | S: Two individuals like to see the noble ones: | T: “Of two people who want to see noble ones—
an7.68:9.2 | S: one likes to hear the true teaching, one does not. | T: one who wants to hear the true Dhamma and one who doesn’t—
an7.68:9.3 | S: The individual who doesn’t like to hear the true teaching | T: the one who doesn’t want to hear the true Dhamma
an7.68:9.4 | S: is reprehensible in that respect. | T: is to be criticized for that reason,
an7.68:9.5 | S: The individual who does like to hear the true teaching | T: the one who does want to hear the true Dhamma is,
an7.68:9.6 | S: is praiseworthy in that respect. | T: for that reason, to be praised.
an7.68:10.1 | S: Two individuals like to hear the true teaching: | T: “Of two people who want to hear the true Dhamma—
an7.68:10.2 | S: one actively listens to the teaching, one does not. | T: one who listens with an attentive ear and one who listens without an attentive ear—
an7.68:10.3 | S: The individual who doesn’t actively listen to the teaching | T: the one who listens without an attentive ear
an7.68:10.4 | S: is reprehensible in that respect. | T: is to be criticized for that reason,
an7.68:10.5 | S: The individual who does actively listen to the teaching | T: the one who listens with an attentive ear is,
an7.68:10.6 | S: is praiseworthy in that respect. | T: for that reason, to be praised.
an7.68:11.1 | S: Two individuals actively listen to the teaching: | T: “Of two people who listen with an attentive ear—
an7.68:11.2 | S: one remembers the teaching they’ve heard, one does not. | T: one who, having listened to the Dhamma, remembers it, and one who doesn’t—
an7.68:11.3 | S: The individual who doesn’t remember the teaching they’ve heard | T: the one who, having listened to the Dhamma, doesn’t remember it
an7.68:11.4 | S: is reprehensible in that respect. | T: is to be criticized for that reason,
an7.68:11.5 | S: The individual who does remember the teaching they’ve heard | T: the one who, having listened to the Dhamma, does remember the Dhamma is,
an7.68:11.6 | S: is praiseworthy in that respect. | T: for that reason, to be praised.
an7.68:12.1 | S: Two individuals remember the teaching they’ve heard: | T: “Of two people who, having listened to the Dhamma, remember it—
an7.68:12.2 | S: one examines the meaning of the teachings they have remembered, one does not. | T: one who explores the meaning of the Dhamma he has remembered and one who doesn’t—
an7.68:12.3 | S: The individual who does not examine the meaning of the teachings they have remembered | T: the one who doesn’t explore the meaning of the Dhamma he has remembered
an7.68:12.4 | S: is reprehensible in that respect. | T: is to be criticized for that reason,
an7.68:12.5 | S: The individual who does examine the meaning of the teachings they have remembered | T: the one who does explore the meaning of the Dhamma he has remembered is,
an7.68:12.6 | S: is praiseworthy in that respect. | T: for that reason, to be praised.
an7.68:13.1 | S: Two individuals examine the meaning of the teachings they have remembered: | T: “Of two people who explore the meaning of the Dhamma they have remembered—
an7.68:13.2 | S: one understands the meaning and the teaching and practicesin line with the teaching, one understands the meaning and the teaching but does not practice in line with the teaching. | T: one who practices the Dhamma in line with the Dhamma, having a sense of Dhamma, having a sense of meaning, and one who doesn’t—
an7.68:13.3 | S: The individual who understands the meaning and the teaching but does not practice in line with the principle of the teaching | T: the one who doesn’t practice the Dhamma in line with the Dhamma, having a sense of Dhamma, having a sense of meaning,
an7.68:13.4 | S: is reprehensible in that respect. | T: is to be criticized for that reason,
an7.68:13.5 | S: The individual who understands the meaning and the teaching and practices in line with the principle of the teaching | T: the one who does practice the Dhamma in line with the Dhamma, having a sense of Dhamma, having a sense of meaning is,
an7.68:13.6 | S: is praiseworthy in that respect. | T: for that reason, to be praised.
an7.68:14.1 | S: Two individuals understand the meaning and the teaching and practice in line with the teaching: | T: “Of two people who practice the Dhamma in line with the Dhamma, having a sense of Dhamma, having a sense of meaning—
an7.68:14.2 | S: one practices to benefit themselves but not others, and one practices to benefit both themselves and others. | T: one who practices for both his own benefit and that of others, and one who practices for his own benefit but not that of others—
an7.68:14.3 | S: The individual who practices to benefit themselves but not others | T: the one who practices for his own benefit but not that of others
an7.68:14.4 | S: is reprehensible in that respect. | T: is to be criticized for that reason,
an7.68:14.5 | S: The individual who practices to benefit both themselves and others | T: the one who practices for both his own benefit and that of others is,
an7.68:14.6 | S: is praiseworthy in that respect. | T: for that reason, to be praised.
an7.68:15.1 | S: That’s how a mendicant understands individuals in terms of pairs. | T: “This is how people are known to a monk in terms of two categories.
an7.68:16.1 | S: That’s how a mendicant is one who knows individuals high and low. | T: And this is how a monk is one with a sense of distinctions among individuals.
an7.68:16.2 | S: A mendicant with these seven qualities is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of veneration with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world.” | T: “A monk endowed with these seven qualities is deserving of gifts, deserving of hospitality, deserving of offerings, deserving of respect, an unexcelled field of merit for the world.”
